      "point": "Both define eschar as dead tissue separated from the skin surface.",
      "quote_britannica": "ESCHAR, in foregry, the craft or fish occationed by home or marine medicine.",
      "quote_wikipedia": "Eschar (/ ˈ ɛ s k ɑːr /; Greek: ἐσχάρᾱ, romanized: eskhara; Latin: eschara; or an eschar) is a slough or piece of dead tissue that is cast off from the surface of the skin"
    }
  ],
  "differences": [
    {
      "point": "Britannica's entry is a brief, garbled definition, while Wikipedia provides a detailed medical explanation.",
      "quote_britannica": "ESCHAR, in foregry, the craft or fish occationed by home or marine medicine.",
      "quote_wikipedia": "Eschar (/ ˈ ɛ s k ɑːr /; Greek: ἐσχάρᾱ, romanized: eskhara; Latin: eschara; or an eschar) is a slough or piece of dead tissue that is cast off from the surface of the skin, particularly after a burn injury"
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Eschar is dead tissue cast off from the surface of the skin.",
      "tag": "enduring",
      "quote_britannica": "ESCHAR, in foregry, the craft or fish occationed by home or marine medicine.",
      "quote_wikipedia": "Eschar (/ ˈ ɛ s k ɑːr /; Greek: ἐσχάρᾱ, romanized: eskhara; Latin: eschara; or an eschar) is a slough or piece of dead tissue that is cast off from the surface of the skin"
    }
  ],
  "term_pairs": [],
  "wiki_only_topics": [
    {
      "topic": "Causes and associations of eschar (burns, gangrene, anthrax, spider bites)",
      "quote_wikipedia": "particularly after a burn injury, but also seen in gangrene, ulcer, fungal infections, necrotizing spider bite wounds, tick bites associated with spotted fevers and exposure to cutaneous anthrax."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Distinction between eschar and scab",
      "quote_wikipedia": "The term ‘eschar’ is not interchangeable with ‘ scab ’. An eschar contains necrotic tissue whereas a scab is composed of dried blood and exudate."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Medical management and escharotomy",
      "quote_wikipedia": "If circulation is compromised, an escharotomy, or surgical incision through the eschar, may be indicated."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Escharotics and their uses",
      "quote_wikipedia": "An escharotic is a substance that kills unwanted or diseased tissue, usually skin or superficial growths like warts, leaving them to slough off."
    }
  ]
}
